  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 3NT. The defense takes the first 4 spade tricks and switches to a heart. South needs all 9 of the remaining tricks. Only 8 tricks are available on top (3 in hearts, 3 in diamonds, and 2 in clubs).\n\nSouth should hope to take a fourth trick in diamonds. It cannot cost to cash South's remaining heart and club winners before playing on diamonds. In doing so, South may be able to get a count on the hand and avoid a third round diamond guess. By playing hearts first, a squeeze might also develop when either defender has length in both diamonds and clubs.\n\nWhen both opponents follow to all of South's heart and club winners, neither of these extra chances will help. South should cash the ♦K and ♦Q. When West follows to the second round of diamonds with the ♦J or ♦10, South must decide whether or not to finesse at trick 12.\n\nThe finesse is necessary when West started with doubleton honour in diamonds. Playing for the drop is necessary when diamonds are 3-3. South should use the Principle of Restricted Choice to guide his decision. This principle is fully explained in hand B-5 on skill level 3.\n\nSouth should finesse, playing for West's choice to have been restricted in his play of his diamond honour. The fact that West played the ♦10 provides an inference that he doesn't have the ♦J. If West had both the ♦J and ♦10 he might have played either honour on the second round of the suit.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠J against 3NT. The defense takes the first 4 spade tricks and switches to a heart. South needs all 9 of the remaining tricks. Only 8 tricks are available on top (3 in hearts, 3 in diamonds, and 2 in clubs).",
    "South should hope to take a fourth trick in diamonds. It cannot cost to cash South's remaining heart and club winners before playing on diamonds. In doing so, South may be able to get a count on the hand and avoid a third round diamond guess. By playing hearts first, a squeeze might also develop when either defender has length in both diamonds and clubs.",
    "When both opponents follow to all of South's heart and club winners, neither of these extra chances will help. South should cash the ♦K and ♦Q. When West follows to the second round of diamonds with the ♦J or ♦10, South must decide whether or not to finesse at trick 12.",
    "The finesse is necessary when West started with doubleton honour in diamonds. Playing for the drop is necessary when diamonds are 3-3. South should use the Principle of Restricted Choice to guide his decision. This principle is fully explained in hand B-5 on skill level 3.",
    "South should finesse, playing for West's choice to have been restricted in his play of his diamond honour. The fact that West played the ♦10 provides an inference that he doesn't have the ♦J. If West had both the ♦J and ♦10 he might have played either honour on the second round of the suit."
